 you cannot locate your Horizon card, you can temporarily freeze it through the Horizon App:\\n  {{faq_7883a8c2_001}}. Sign in to the Horizon App.\\n  {{faq_7883a8c2_002}}. On the card overview screen, tap \\\"View & Manage Card.\\\"\\n  {{faq_7883a8c2_003}}. Select \\\"Freeze Card.\\\"\\n- For a card that is lost or stolen, first freeze it and then request a replacement:\\n  {{faq_7883a8c2_004}}. Log into the Horizon App.\\n  {{faq_7883a8c2_005}}. Open the card dashboard and choose \\\"View & Manage Card.\\\"\\n  {{faq_7883a8c2_006}}. Tap \\\"Freeze Card\\\" followed by \\\"Order Replacement.\\\"\\n  {{faq_7883a8c2_007}}. Horizon will issue a new card and permanently deactivate the old one.\\n- You may also wish to file a police report.\\n- The new card will arrive with a different card number; be sure to update any recurring payments (e.g., subscriptions) with the new details.\\n- For additional assistance, select \\\"Contact Us\\\" from the app menu.\"}, {\"parent_id\": \"faq_98eb6af4\", \"content\": \"Question: I'm currently using my Horizon Link Credit/Debit Card, but my Horizon App displays the NTUC Link Credit/Debit Card. How can I obtain a physical version of the NTUC Link Credit/Debit Card?\\n\\nAnswer: To receive a physical NTUC Link Credit/Debit Card, you'll need to initiate a lost card replacement process through the Horizon App. It's essential to choose the correct replacement option, as selecting 'damaged card' will not yield the desired outcome. Please be aware that replacing your card will result in changes to your card details, including the card number, CVV, and expiry date. Consequently, you will need to update your card information for any existing online credit card arrangements, such as card-on-file and recurring payments, to ensure uninterrupted service.\"}]}, {\"task_id\": \"task_2\", \"resolved_request\": \"How do I change my registered address with the bank?\", \"approved_parents\": [{\"parent_id\": \"faq_11305460\", \"content\": \"Question: How can I change personal information such as my NRIC/FIN, nationality, name, or the name embossed on my Horizon card?\\n\\nAnswer: - To modify details like NRIC/FIN, nationality, legal name, or the card‑embossing name, reach out to Horizon Customer Care via the Horizon app’s call or chat function. Expect a response within roughly three business days.\"}]}]\n    Intent clarification inputs: []\n    Terminal unresolved task markers (topic/status only; never evidence): [{\"task_id\": \"task_2\", \"type\": \"TERMINAL_RETRIEVAL_FEEDBACK\", \"resolved_subquery\": \"How do I change my registered address with the bank?\", \"resolution_status\": \"PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT\"}]\n    Decline inputs: []\n    Pending service requests (a guided form has already been registered for\n    each; you do not know and must not invent its fields): [{\"special_id\": \"YgrFtTSskZ8xRwyoSG9inrL3eLRZsiSD\", \"service_request_id\": \"profile-address-update\", \"description\": \"Submit a new postal address for the customer's profile.\"}]\n    Unavailable service requests (could not be registered this turn; state\n    only the supplied reason, never guess why): []\n    Failed outbound-audit feedback (constraints only, never evidence). Each\n    entry identifies one specific claim or instruction to remove — it is\n    never permission to abandon the topic it came from. After removing\n    exactly what is flagged, re-apply every Design Principle below to still\n    give the most complete, honestly-scoped response the remaining approved\n    evidence supports:\n    [\"Compliance audit failed: Remove the instruction to complete a form for submitting a new postal address. State clearly and neutrally that the information on reporting a lost card and changing a registered address is currently unavailable, without inviting direct profile modification actions through the assistant.\", \"Compliance audit failed: Remove the instruction to complete a form for updating the postal address and ensure the response only provides neutral, factual information without implying banking actions or workflows.\"]\n\n    \nPRECISION REFERENCE CONTRACT:\n- A token matching `{{faq_*}}` is a database-backed exact-value reference,\n  never missing, unknown, incomplete, or a customer-visible template.\n- It may represent an exact amount, rate, limit, date, threshold, or other\n  precise value. Treat it as the underlying approved fact for relevance,\n  sufficiency, generation, compliance, and groundedness.\n- Preserve the token exactly in any draft. The backend deterministically\n  substitutes its authoritative value only after outbound validation.\n- Never reject, weaken, or describe evidence as unavailable merely because the\n  exact value is represented by a `{{faq_*}}` token.\n- Within the stated scope, \"up to {{faq_*}}\" expresses an exact maximum. It\n  supports the bounded conclusion that the stated coverage or benefit does not\n  exceed that value. It does not, by itself, support claims about applications,\n  approvals, denials, exceptions, or who controls the limit.\n\n\n    Design principles:\n    - Core job: first understand the whole current customer situation from the\n      complete Customer request and Trusted conversation context. Then decide\n      which approved evidence, if any, meaningfully addresses that situation.\n      Write one natural, faithful response to that whole picture; do not merely\n      render a retrieval task or a UI recommendation.\n    - Source authority: the Customer request and Trusted conversation context\n      establish what the customer means, refers to, and cares about. Approved\n      evidence is the only source for banking facts, procedures, policies,\n      conditions, options, and UI content. User baseline only calibrates tone\n      when clearly relevant. Global resolution context never overrides the\n      current customer situation or evidence scope.\n    - Applicability comes before presentation. An approved record may describe\n     